\section{Why \code{dRange} has to be given explicitly}{


Left to itself, \code{\link[surveillance:nowcast]{surveillance::nowcast()}} infers the time axis from the data
it was handed -- and a \strong{line list cannot express a zero}. A day on which
nothing was reported has no rows, so it is not in the line list, so it is not
in the inferred axis. That is exactly the situation at the \code{now} edge, which
is the part you are nowcasting: the last few days are quiet precisely because
their reports have not arrived yet, and the axis silently stops short of
\code{now}. Passing \code{dRange} states the grid instead of letting it be guessed, so
the quiet days at the end are modelled as zeros observed so far rather than
as days that do not exist.

This is also why \code{\link[=complete_zeroes]{complete_zeroes()}} is no help here: it can only add zero
\emph{counts}, and a line list has no count column to put a zero in.
